task: actor-receive-offer-first (channel-callback-allocation's receive half, for the actor loop)
agent: okay-42
started: 2026-09-06
branch: feature/actor-receive-offer-first

why: actorTell reads 1.49x the bare mailbox shape (295.9 vs 198.5us,
  section 17), and JFR names it per message: Slot 142, Right 95, Some
  89, the block lambda 87, the Await 87. The loop calls
  receiveBlocking() once per message -- on purpose, so supervision
  knows which message failed -- and pays the full handshake even when
  the message is already buffered, which under load it always is. The
  exact mirror of feed-offer-first (-38% on the send side).

how: a synchronous poll on Channel -- receiveNow -- answered from the
  ring with no Slot, no Await and no Right(Some(_)) pair, distinguishing
  "an element", "nothing yet" and "the end / a failure". The actor loop
  tries it first and falls back to receiveBlocking() only on "nothing
  yet". One message per poll, so the one-at-a-time property that
  supervision rests on is untouched.

laws: a poll takes exactly one; close, fail and the end mark are seen
  through the poll as through receive; a poll never overtakes a parked
  receiver (fairness with waiters, as offer refuses while a sender is
  parked); the default implementation on Channel answers "nothing yet"
  so every other channel stays correct before it is fast.

measure: actorTell time and bytes, JFR before/after; the Slot count is
  the proof, the ratio to channelBuffer the claim.
